WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:06.740
There are extensive delays and suspensions
in train service

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.620
because of a temporary loss of power.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:15.140
The New York City subway is the circulatory
system for the global capitol of finance and media.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:20.580
And today this 114 year old engineering marvel
is coming apart.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.440
Stalled trains, signal breakdowns and constant
line closures

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.620
are complicating the lives on New Yorkers

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.880
who ride the trains more than 5.5 million
times a day.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.900
The MTA, the public agency that runs the subways
is woefully mismanaged,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.220
fiscally irresponsible and politically captured.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.540
Thanks to the clout of the transit workers
union,

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:47.899
subway workers on average make 155 thousand
dollars in total annual compensation

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.800
or more than twice as much as the passengers
they serve.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.740
The political response to this crisis

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:58.570
has been mainly to devise new ways to collect
more money for this troubled operation

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:04.700
such as a new millionaires tax or by imposing
additional tolls and surcharges on cars.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.160
But a major lesson of the first 114 years
of subway history

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.740
is that giving the MTA more money from outside
sources

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.620
is like bringing an alcoholic to an open bar.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.079
The path to real reform and accountability

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.080
is to make the subway live off its customers
once and for all.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.539
Subway riders should bear almost the full
cost to ride

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.420
with the exception of low income folks where
there's going to be some subsidy.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:35.820
Baruch Feigenbaum is the assistant director
of transportation policy at the Reason Foundation.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.340
A fare in New York City is a couple bucks
and change,

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.720
what would it be if it was more fully account?

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.759
So during rush hours it would probably be
at least five dollars one way

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.400
and probably up to eight or maybe even ten
based on how far the person is travelling.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.800
We charge what it takes to cover the cost

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.180
and then for folks who need the service,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.279
they would get a voucher or some sort of other
discount so they can use it

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.120
we're not trying to subsidize upper middle
class folks here.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.020
One of the arguments against that is it's
gonna decrease economic activity overall

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.740
because there is a positive externality

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.630
to subsidizing people who come into Manhattan
from the boroughs.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.400
I really don't think eight or ten dollars
even is that much

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.150
when you consider some of the other challenges
in New York and people still live there

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.940
The center of everything does not have to
be Manhattan.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.140
Is that the limit of transit policy?

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.500
That people ride, we pay the full cost?

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:33.360
That is the most efficient system, it's also
the best system for the rider.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.400
Because if the rider is the one paying the
cost

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.520
then the transit agency is serving the rider.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.160
If Albany is bailing out the transit system,
it's gonna be whatever Albany wants.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.060
Lately whatever Albany wants has included
budget items big and small

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.920
that don't have any impact on service and
reliability.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.040
And this is a theme that extends all the way
back to the system's early days.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.420
The subway's troubles are deeply rooted in
the decline of the fare

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.740
as it's primary revenue source.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.160
When the subway opened in 1904 it was five
cents a ride.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.960
Which was more than enough to finance its
operations.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.260
But when inflation eroded the value of a nickel,
the city refused to permit fare increases.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:32.520
By 1920, 500 other US cities had raised fares
on their transit systems.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.720
Meanwhile New York City's populist mayor John
Hylan

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.440
campaigned on the preservation of the nickel
fare,

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.220
calling it 'as sacred and binding as any contracts
ever drawn

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.880
in the history of financial transactions the
world over.'

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.840
The fare did eventually go up but not enough
to keep pace with inflation

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.360
and the subway's revenue shortages became
an endemic problem.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.120
Decades later the city and state discovered
a new way to plug up the shortfall

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.680
forcing drivers to pay for transit.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.920
In 1968, governor Nelson Rockefeller orchestrated
a backroom deal

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.060
to reallocate revenues from nine major bridges
and tunnels to transit.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.980
Last year alone drivers payed 1.1 billion
dollars in tolls

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.700
that were diverted to subways and buses.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.960
The state created more hidden funding streams
in the years that followed

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.480
and taxes and subsidies now comprise a larger
share of the MTA's revenue than fares.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.499
These include a special sales, corporate and
payroll tax

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:39.480
and fees on real estate transfers, car rentals,
driver's license renewals and vehicle registrations.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.760
And the state just imposed a brand new surcharge
on taxis and ride shares coming into Manhattan

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.700
with the money going to the MTA.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.740
These subsidies violate a central tenet of
good transportation policy.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.900
Which is to make each mode of travel self
sustaining.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.960
If fare subsidies were to come to an end,

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.080
could the subway survive on its customers alone?

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.820
It could in 1904 when the system opened,

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.210
and it could in 1982 when the MTA studied
the issue

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.460
and journalist Peter Samuel wrote about it
in Reason.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:14.320
The key is getting riders directly invested
in slashing wasteful spending

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.749
and reigning in wildly inflated salaries

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:20.820
by making them feel the horror not just of
signal malfunctions,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.920
train rerouting and line closures,

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.440
but that they pay top dollar for the privilege
of such dreadful service.

